{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "308",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 70s presented with a pruritic and painful periorbital eruption that had lasted 1 month. Her medical history included lupus nephritis requiring hemodialysis, immune thrombocytopenia, acute myeloid leukemia, chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy, deep vein thrombosis, diverticulitis, and postcolectomy diarrhea. Past treatments included hydroxychloroquine (resulted in retinal toxic effects) and lenalidomide (severely worsened platelet count, with a nadir of 28 ×103/μL).\n\nPhysical examination demonstrated scaly, crusted pink plaques that involved the periorbital skin.\n\n- Biopsy Results: Punch biopsy from the left upper eyelid demonstrated vacuolar interface dermatitis with follicular involvement and epidermal atrophy.- Images:  Image Title: Figure 1. Frontal View of Periorbital Lesions Before and After 2 Months of Treatment  Image Description: Frontal view showing periorbital involvement    Image Title: Figure 2. Views of Periorbital Lesions Before and After 2 Months of Treatment  Image Description: Views from the right and left sides",
